@article{ WOS:001589709500002,
Author = {Okioma, Susan and Ngunjiri, Anne S. W. and Davis, Katy and Akinyi,
   Pacific and Aktar, Bachera and Arjyal, Abriti and Dacombe, Russell and
   Gaitho, Michael and Guma, Consolata and Kwabla, Mavis and Limato,
   Ralalicia and Mavhu, Webster and Monga, Meenakshi and Mwaniki, Anthony
   and Paul, Sneha and Ramsay, Andrew and Rashid, Sabina Faiz and Theobald,
   Sally and Steege, Rosie},
Title = {Equity, data and the surveillance gap in antimicrobial resistance: A
   call for inclusive action},
Journal = {CABI ONE HEALTH},
Year = {2025},
Volume = {4},
Number = {1},
Abstract = {Antimicrobial resistance (AMR) is a growing global health concern that
   spans human, animal, and environmental health, making it a
   quintessential One Health issue. Global AMR surveillance systems such as
   the World Health Organisation's (WHO) Global Antimicrobial Resistance
   and Use Surveillance System (GLASS) have created important opportunities
   for countries to collect and report AMR data systematically across
   sectors and settings. These systems also include the capacity to capture
   variables such as sex, age, and infection origin (community-acquired or
   healthcare-associated). This can facilitate the use of equity indicators
   and provide a foundation for more inclusive and responsive AMR analysis.
   This commentary highlights the value of building on these existing
   systems by expanding the routine collection and analysis of both basic
   and advanced equity-related variables and indicators using the human
   health sector as an entry point. Incorporating variables such as
   location of residence, education level, occupation, socio-economic
   status, disability, and ethnicity enables an intersectional analysis.
   This can enhance understanding of who is most affected by AMR and why,
   and inform effective responses. Recognising that AMR surveillance data
   often comes from health facilities, we also explore the benefits of
   complementing it with community-level and community-led approaches to
   better reflect diverse realities and patterns ofAMR across populations
   and contexts. By integrating equity considerations into AMR data
   systems, countries and global stakeholders can strengthen the design,
   implementation, and targeting of interventions. These steps support more
   responsive health systems and contribute to AMR solutions that are not
   only scientifically sound but also socially inclusive. Embedding equity
   in AMR surveillance is a vital step toward achieving the full promise of
   One Health-ensuring that responses are informed, effective, and
   reflective of the diverse communities they are meant to serve. One
   Health impact statement Antimicrobial resistance (AMR) is a
   quintessential One Health issue, shaped by the interconnected health of
   humans, animals, and the environment. This commentary highlights how
   equity-centred human health surveillance can enhance AMR responses by
   improving our understanding of who is most affected and why. We discuss
   human health surveillance as an entry point as it aligns with the
   expertise of the authorship group, who bring reflections from working
   across the Fleming Fund's (2025) global portfolio under Gender and
   Equity within Antimicrobial Resistance (GEAR up) (GEAR up, 2024). GEAR
   up is a regional grant funded by the Fleming Fund that seeks to catalyse
   action on equity within AMR through mainstreaming gender and equity
   within routine AMR systems and structures. Aligned with One Health as a
   concept, strengthening the routine collection and use of disaggregated
   data-such as sex, age, and socio-economic status-can improve the design
   of inclusive, targeted interventions across sectors. The commentary
   draws on transdisciplinary collaboration involving public health
   experts, social scientists, implementers, and policy makers from
   multiple countries. Social science perspectives augment and improve our
   understanding of AMR beyond purely biomedical terms, improving
   understanding of how social and cultural nuances influence perceptions
   towards, and responses to it.
   By integrating social science perspectives alongside epidemiological and
   microbiological expertise, this approach detects often-overlooked
   inequities and promotes context-sensitive action. It also underscores
   the importance of combining academic and pragmatic knowledge systems to
   co-produce insights that are directly relevant to communities and
   decision makers. Ultimately, this work supports more effective and
   inclusive surveillance systems, contributing to improved health
   outcomes, stronger accountability, and more equitable global AMR
   responses.},
