Title = {Paternal Affection, Masculinity, and Fathers' Parenting Self-Efficacy:
   Findings From an Australian Cohort Study},
Journal = {SEX ROLES},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {92},
Number = {5},
Month = {AUG 25},
Abstract = {Parenting self-efficacy refers to the confidence that parents have in
   their ability to successfully promote children's development and to
   overcome parenting challenges. Yet how parenting self-efficacy unfolds
   in men remains largely unknown. Developmental ecological models of
   fathering suggest that fathers' parenting beliefs, such as parenting
   self-efficacy, develop through a complex interaction of factors. In the
   present study, we examine two important individual factors that may
   influence parenting self-efficacy: men's recollection of affection
   received from their own father and men's cultural values regarding
   traditional masculinity. Data were obtained from the Australian
   Longitudinal Study on Male Health (Ten to Men), Wave 1 and Wave 2 of the
   adult age group (N = 7,901). After adjustment for covariates and prior
   depression, we found that higher levels of recalled paternal affection
   were significantly associated with greater parenting self-efficacy.
   Masculine norms relating to `winning' and `risk taking' were positively
   associated with greater parenting efficacy, while `emotional control,'
   `violence,' `playboy,' and `power over women' were associated with lower
   parenting self-efficacy. Our findings suggest that fathers' affection
   has long-term consequences for their children's future parenting
   self-efficacy. To enhance their parenting self-efficacy, some men may
   benefit from support that challenges the idealisation of masculine
   dominance while reinforcing evolving expectations of fathers.},
